.TH DRAWINK 1
.SH NAME
drawink \- check that drawing reaches the pixels
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B drawink
[
.B -k
] [
.I dev
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Drawink
draws geometry of known size and colour into the screen image and then
reads it back
.I out of the screen image,
so the pixels are checked where they actually landed rather than where
the drawing code thought it put them.
It reports a line per check and exits with a non-empty status if any
check failed, so a script can gate on it.
.PP
.I Dev
is the directory holding the draw and pointer devices, by default
.BR /dev ;
.B drawink
binds
.B #i
and
.B #m
after it, which is harmless if they are already there.
.PP
The checks are:
.TP
.B clear
A screen-wide fill with black, read back one row: every byte must be
zero.
.TP
.B box inside
A rectangle covering the middle half of the screen, filled red: every
pixel strictly inside must be non-zero.
.TP
.B box outside
The band above that rectangle must still be all zero.  A row-stride
error leaves the inside of a fill correct and smears the outside, so
this is the check that catches it.
.TP
.B halves
Two abutting full-height fills, green and white, sampled on the middle
row: each half must be entirely inked.  A stride error paints the second
fill over the first.
.TP
.B text ink
A string drawn with the built-in font must leave some, but not all, of
its bounding box inked.  Text goes through memdraw as a mask rather than
as a solid source, so a glyph path that lands nowhere is a failure none
of the colour fills can see.
The built-in font needs no
.BR /fonts ,
so this runs on a board with no filesystem; the check is skipped if the
font cannot be opened.
.TP
.B "undrawn region"
Three presents \(em all white, all green, then a small box in a corner
\(em and after the third the strip far from that box must still read
exactly the bytes the green fill left.  This is the acceptance test for
double buffering with incremental drawing: with two buffers the back one
holds the frame before last, so everything a frame does not redraw has to
be carried forward before the flip, and if that copy is missing the drawn
region is still perfectly correct and only the UNDRAWN region is wrong.
None of the checks above looks there \(em every one of them reads where
ink was just put.
If the strip reads the bytes of the WHITE fill instead, the message says
.B "TWO FRAMES STALE"
and the buffer is the one from before last, which is the exact signature
of a missing or no-op forward copy.
The actual bytes of each fill are captured rather than assumed, so no
channel order is presumed; if white and green happen to read alike the
check reports SKIP instead of passing.
.PP
.B "This check passes trivially with a single buffer,"
and that is said rather than hidden.  It can only fail when double
buffering is enabled and wrong, which is what it is for.  So a PASS is not
evidence that double buffering is on, and on its own it is not evidence the
check works either.
.PP
.B "There is a control for it,"
and it is the same idea as
.B -k
below.
.I os/inferno/raspi3b/screenbcm.c
has
.B Dbufnorepair ,
which makes the forward copy a no-op \(em reproducing exactly the bug the
first version of that copy had, an inverted ternary that copied the back
buffer onto itself.  With
.B Dbuf
on and
.B Dbufnorepair
on, this check MUST fail and print
.BR "TWO FRAMES STALE" .
If it does not, the check is watching the wrong region and proves nothing,
which is the trap a test of an undrawn region falls into most easily.
.PP
.B clear
and
.B box inside
are each other's control: the same pixels must read all zero after the
clear and all non-zero after the fill, so neither a readback stuck at
zero nor one stuck at full scale can pass both.
.PP
When a check fails,
.B drawink
also prints the bounding box of the pixels that disagreed.
A kernel with a software cursor composites a 16x16 overlay into the
screen image, which on a direct framebuffer reads back as holes in a
solid fill \- the cursor working, not the fill failing \-
so
.B drawink
installs an all-zero cursor while it runs and restores the default on
the way out.
A failure in a 16x16 box at the pointer therefore means a cursor that
did not get hidden; anything else is a drawing fault.
.PP
The
.B -k
flag leaves the cursor alone.  On a kernel that composites one into the
screen image it is expected to fail, and that is its purpose: it is how
these checks are shown to be capable of failing at all, and a plain run
followed by a
.B -k
run that sees the cursor again is what verifies the restore.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/drawink.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR draw (3),
.IR pointer (3),
.IR draw-image (2)
.SH BUGS
Reading pixels back is only as good as the draw device's own readback
path: a fault common to both the write and the read would pass.  On
a Raspberry Pi 3B
with a direct framebuffer the readback comes out of video RAM, so the
check does reach the memory the GPU scans out; on a kernel with a
shadow framebuffer it stops at the shadow, and says nothing about
whether the copy to the display happened.
.PP
What has actually been run.  The first five checks pass on a hosted
.IR emu (1)
under MacOS X at 1024x768 x8b8g8r8, and on a Raspberry Pi 3B on silicon
at 640x480 x8r8g8b8 reading back out of scanout RAM.  The two agree on
the glyph ink to the pixel (226 of 1728 inked, ink box 9,10 to 71,21).
.PP
On that board, with the cursor enabled,
.B -k
fails two checks and names a 14x14 box at 320,240 \- the pointer hotpoint
the kernel seeds to the screen centre \- reproducing 75539 of 75684 and
306 of 320 exactly.  So the checks discriminate, and the plain run that
precedes it put the default cursor back.
.PP
.B "The undrawn-region check has NOT been run against a double-buffered kernel,"
because there is not one yet.
.I os/inferno/raspi3b/screenbcm.c
has the page flip and the forward copy written and switched off
.RB ( Dbuf
is 0), waiting on the board.  Against the single-buffered kernel the
check passes, which as described above demonstrates only that it does not
false-alarm.  The control
.RB ( Dbuf
on,
.B Dbufnorepair
on, the check expected to FAIL) has not been run either, because that also
needs the board.  Until both arms have been run on hardware, treat this
check as written rather than as verified.
